@meta
id: 28-08
title: Two lines that replace a file picker
chapter: 28
type: lesson
skills: vimrc-structure
par: 138
minutes: 6
vscode: Ctrl-P
open: .vimrc
vimrc: set path=.,** | set wildmenu | set wildmode=longest:full,full

@brief
Chapter 18 gave you `:find`, and it was underwhelming, because out of the
box `path` is `.,/usr/include,,` — the directory of the current file, and
C headers. Two lines fix that for good:

    set path=.,**             " :find searches the whole tree
    set wildmenu              " Tab completion shows a menu, not a guess
    set wildmode=longest:full,full

`**` means "every directory below the working directory, recursively". With
it, `:find order.rep` and Tab opens `src/domain/order/order.repository.ts`
without you knowing or typing the path. `wildmenu` puts the candidates on a
bar above the command line; `wildmode=longest:full,full` says the first Tab
completes as far as the candidates agree, and the next one starts stepping
through them.

Add the three lines to the Editing section of your config. Then use them:
somewhere under `src/` there is a repository with a `findMany` that has no
`take`. Open it with `:find`, not with a path you typed out, and give the
query `take: 100,` under `where`.

The settings are already live in this session, so `:find` works before you
save the config.

@keys
| `:find name` `Tab` | open a file by name from anywhere in the tree |
| `:find *fragment` `Tab` | match a fragment of the name, not just a prefix |
| `:ls` | which buffers you have |
| `:wqa` | write everything and quit |

@hints
Config first: the three lines go in the Editing section, under
`set backspace`. Then `:w`.
--
`:find order.repo` and press Tab. With `path=.,**` the whole tree is in
scope, so the directory does not have to be part of what you type.
--
Inside the `findMany` call, put the cursor on the `where:` line and `o`
gives you a correctly indented new line. Then `:wqa` writes both files.

@solution
    /set backspace<Enter> o set path=.,**<Enter> set wildmenu<Enter>
    set wildmode=longest:full,full <Esc> :w<Enter>
    :find order.repo<Tab><Enter>
    /where:<Enter> o take: 100, <Esc> :wqa<Enter>

Everything after the first `:w` is the config doing the work. You never
typed `src/domain/order/`.

`path` is worth more than the keystrokes it saves, because several other
commands read it. `gf` on a filename under the cursor uses `path`.
`:sfind` opens into a split. `:vimgrep /pat/ **/*.ts` is a different
mechanism but the same habit: stop navigating directories, start naming
files.

The one warning: `**` walks the whole tree from your working directory,
so in a repository with a 400MB `node_modules` the first Tab takes a
noticeable moment. `set wildignore=*/node_modules/**,*/dist/**,*/.git/**`
keeps those out of completion, and out of `:find`. That line is the fourth
one you add to this section, on the day it first annoys you.

@start .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Editing ---
set hidden
set backspace=indent,eol,start

@expected .vimrc
" ~/.vimrc - one line at a time, when something annoys you.

set nocompatible
syntax on
filetype plugin indent on

" --- Editing ---
set hidden
set backspace=indent,eol,start
set path=.,**
set wildmenu
set wildmode=longest:full,full

@start src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(args: FindManyArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: args.where,
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
    });
  }
}

@expected src/domain/order/order.repository.ts
import { injectable } from 'tsyringe';

@injectable()
export class OrderRepository {
  public async findMany(args: FindManyArgs) {
    return this.prisma.order.findMany({
      where: args.where,
      take: 100,
      orderBy: { placedAt: 'desc' },
    });
  }
}
